<noscript><p>A: control, but engineering risk. B: speed, but connector and fit risk. C: my starting choice; it must be time-boxed to avoid delaying useful delivery.</p></noscript></article><article class="lab-slide" aria-label="2 of 7">
<p class="lab-step">Evidence & assumptions</p><h2 class="dilemma__title">Size the problem.<br>Test the assumptions.</h2>
<div class="insight-stack">
<section class="insight"><span class="insight-label">External evidence · 2025</span><h3><strong>57%</strong> cited data silos as a barrier to using lab data.</h3><p>Start with access to evidence and fragmented knowledge.</p><details><summary>Source and limits</summary><p><a href="https://pistoiaalliance.org/news/survey-ai-adoption-life-sciences-labs-skills-gap/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pistoia Alliance · September 2025 ↗</a>. Survey of 200+ experts across life sciences and related sectors. Not a representative sample of 2,000-person biotechs; it does not establish this company’s baseline.</p></details></section>
<section class="insight"><span class="insight-label">External evidence · 2024</span><h3><strong>43%</strong> of large-biopharma IT respondents supported 20+ scientific applications.</h3><p>Integration and source coverage belong in the business case.</p><details><summary>Source and limits</summary><p><a href="https://www.benchling.com/blog/2024-state-of-tech-report" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Benchling · November 2024 ↗</a>. Survey of 300 R&D/IT respondents in the US and Europe; “large” meant 1,000+ employees. Vendor-sponsored, self-reported evidence. The percentage applies to the large-biopharma IT subgroup.</p></details></section>
<section class="insight insight--assumption"><span class="insight-label">Planning assumption · validate in discovery</span><h3><strong>5–7 hours/week</strong> searching and collecting information per scientist.</h3><p>Use 6 hours as a working scenario—not a measured average.</p><details><summary>How I would validate it</summary><p>Measure search and collection separately from waiting, synthesis and correction. Use a two-week baseline across relevant workflows. The cited research supports the fragmentation problem; it does not establish this weekly search-time assumption.</p></details></section>
</div></article>
<article class="lab-slide" aria-label="3 of 7">
<p class="lab-step">Discovery & experiment</p><h2 class="dilemma__title">Find the friction.<br>Fund a bounded test.</h2>
<div class="insight-stack">
<section class="insight"><span class="insight-label">Understand the workflow</span><h3><strong>12 scientists · 3 departments</strong></h3><p>Observe real work and measure two weeks of friction before choosing the solution.</p><details><summary>Discovery method</summary><p>Log search, waiting, synthesis and correction separately. Trace ELN/LIMS, file shares and expert handoffs. Test whether access rights, metadata or missing records cause delays. This is proposed discovery, not completed findings.</p></details></section>
<section class="insight"><span class="insight-label">Compare against a useful alternative</span><h3><strong>8 weeks · 30 scientists · 2 sources</strong></h3><p>One candidate-review workflow. Compare AI with improved search and the current workflow.</p><details><summary>Pilot design and evaluation</summary><p>Use committed scientists and approved sources. Compare source-linked AI, improved non-AI search and the current workflow on matched tasks in randomized order. Include difficult and unanswerable questions; scientists score usefulness blind where practical. Measure net time after review and correction, answer quality and access-control failures.</p></details></section>

</div></article><article class="lab-slide" aria-label="4 of 7">
<p class="lab-step">Market scan</p><h2 class="dilemma__title">Buy, adapt or build?</h2>
<p class="business-intro">Compare equivalent scope: source coverage, permissions, useful answers and ongoing ownership.</p>
