</figure>
<div class="business-verdict"><strong>What earns the next investment?</strong><p>Validate sustained adoption and net time saved. Test reuse with a second team. Do not add milestone gains to annual capacity: the same benefit may be counted twice.</p></div>
<details class="lab-more"><summary>Assumptions, calculation and limits</summary>
<p>Annual capacity = 600 scientists × 46 weeks × adoption × (weekly search hours × reduction − added weekly review hours). Cautious: 40%, 5h, 15%, 0.5h = 2,760h. Working: 60%, 6h, 25%, 0.5h = 16,560h. Upside: 80%, 7h, 35%, 0.4h = 45,264h. Scenario points use unrounded values on a zero-based scale; labels are rounded.</p>
<p>The 5–7h baseline and 15–35% reduction are assumptions, not study averages or pilot results. At an assumed €70/hour, gross capacity value is about €0.2m / €1.2m / €3.2m before ongoing costs. This is opportunity cost, not payroll savings, revenue or financial return. Budget is only released through an explicit redeployment or spending decision.</p>
<p>Reuse assumes connectors, access rules, evidence templates and evaluation sets transfer to a new product line of 100 scientists. A 16-week evidence-workflow baseline could become 10–14 weeks. New data formats, scientific entities or permissions could eliminate the gain.</p>
<p>A 24-week research milestone could shorten by 1–2 weeks if four weeks of evidence preparation sit on the critical path and fall by 25–50%. If wet-lab work or approvals dominate, milestone gain is zero. Each reuse row has its own zero-based weeks scale. Blue ranges show assumed outcomes, not forecasts.</p>
<p>Validate adoption, search time and review cost in the pilot. Test transfer to a second team. Do not add milestone gains to annual capacity: the same benefit may be counted twice.</p>
</details></article><article class="lab-slide" aria-label="6 of 7">
<p class="lab-step">My recommendation</p><h2 class="dilemma__title">Fund learning.<br>Earn the next commitment.</h2>
<div class="funding-card"><span>Proposed initial commitment</span><strong>€80–150k</strong><p>8-week pilot · 30 scientists · 2 approved sources</p><p>4–5 FTE-equivalent · senior engineering required</p></div>
<ol class="funding-path" aria-label="Staged investment decision">
<li><span class="funding-dot">1</span><div><strong>Secure the foundations</strong><p>Approved access, a senior integration engineer and committed scientists.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="funding-dot">2</span><div><strong>Test against a real alternative</strong><p>Compare an existing-estate vendor with improved search on the same scientific tasks.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="funding-dot">3</span><div><strong>Decide at week eight</strong><p>Use measured value, quality and ownership costs to decide the next commitment.</p></div></li>
</ol>
<section class="gate-scorecard" aria-label="Proposed funding gates"><h3>Evidence required for more investment</h3><div><strong>≥20%</strong><span>Net preparation-time reduction vs improved search</span></div><div><strong>Quality</strong><span>No material quality loss; verifiable support for material claims</span></div><div><strong>Access</strong><span>No unauthorized disclosure in adversarial tests</span></div></section>
<div class="funding-outcomes"><div><strong>Pass →</strong><span>Scope the next investment</span></div><div><strong>Unclear ↻</strong><span>Run a narrower test</span></div><div><strong>Fail ■</strong><span>Stop if review effort erases gains</span></div></div>
<p class="scenario-note">Proposed gates, not reported results. Report task/user spread and uncertainty. No rollout commitment before the evidence review.</p>
<details class="lab-more"><summary>Pilot cost breakdown and team requirements</summary><p>Proposed team: 4–5 FTE-equivalent across a product/domain lead, senior integration engineer, data engineer, data scientist/evaluation lead and fractional security, UX and scientist time.</p><p>Planning calculation: 4–5 FTE-equivalent × 2 months × €10–14k per FTE-month, plus €0–10k tools, rounded to €80–150k. Secure senior engineering capacity before starting; data science capacity does not replace software engineering.</p><p>This is a pilot estimate, not a supplier quote or rollout budget. The next investment depends on the week-eight evidence review.</p></details>
<details class="lab-more"><summary>Accountability and ownership</summary><p>Scientists own usefulness and review. Data owners own access and metadata. Engineering owns supportability. Finance challenges capacity assumptions. The CEO sponsors the next decision, not a preferred answer.</p><p>Choose a hybrid only where a demonstrated gap justifies ownership. Agree the next scope and its budget after the pilot; the initial €80–150k is not a full-rollout cost.</p></details>
</article><article class="lab-slide" aria-label="7 of 7">
<p class="lab-step">Custom solution · conditional plan</p><h2 class="dilemma__title">From pilot evidence<br>to a product worth owning.</h2>
<p class="scenario-caption">Illustrative internal-product model · no pilot results assumed</p>
<div class="business-verdict"><strong>Only proceed if the experiment earns it</strong><p>Meet the pilot’s value, quality and access gates—and demonstrate a repeatable scientific need that vendors cannot meet economically. A successful AI pilot alone does not justify a custom build.</p></div>
<div class="insight-stack">
<section class="insight"><span class="insight-label">Product vision</span><h3>Trustworthy scientific evidence, ready for the next research decision.</h3><p>Help scientists create source-linked candidate-review briefs across approved knowledge sources.</p><details><summary>Users, value and product boundaries</summary><p>Serve scientists and research decision owners. Connect evidence to its source, preserve permissions and make uncertainty visible. Measure net preparation time, brief quality and repeat use. Start with one candidate-review workflow; autonomous scientific decisions and foundation-model training are outside scope.</p></details></section>
<section class="insight"><span class="insight-label">Strategy</span><h3>Own the scientific workflow. Reuse the technology.</h3><p>Build only the differentiated layer; use existing models and retrieval components.</p><details><summary>Choices, dependencies and success measures</summary><p>Own evidence templates, workflow, evaluations and integrations where the pilot proves vendor gaps. Use existing models and reusable retrieval tools. Prioritize permission-aware access, traceability and supportability over feature breadth. Commit scientific owners, senior engineering and data owners before delivery.</p><p>Continue only while net preparation time improves by at least 20% versus improved search without material quality loss, material claims remain verifiable and adversarial tests show no unauthorized disclosure. Review ongoing quality, adoption, incidents and full ownership cost quarterly. Stop or change approach if benefits fail to persist.</p></details></section>
<section class="insight"><span class="insight-label">3-year roadmap</span><h3>Prove → transfer → scale selectively</h3>
<div class="roadmap-years"><div><strong>Year 1</strong><b>Prove</b><span>One workflow<br>2 approved sources<br>30% adoption*</span></div><div><strong>Year 2</strong><b>Transfer</b><span>Second-team test<br>Reusable connectors<br>50% adoption*</span></div><div><strong>Year 3</strong><b>Scale</b><span>Approved workflows<br>Reliable operations<br>60% adoption*</span></div></div>
<details><summary>Release gates and sequencing</summary><p>Year 1: establish the permission model, source-linked briefs and evaluation suite; release in stages after the pilot. Year 2: test transfer to a second team before extending source coverage. Measure reuse effort and time to first trustworthy brief. Year 3: expand only to workflows that demonstrate repeatable value; strengthen monitoring, support and cost controls.</p><p>*Illustrative average annual adoption across 600 eligible scientists, not commitments or end-of-year targets. Adoption requires phased rollout beyond the original 30 pilot participants. Each year’s funding is conditional on evidence; no automatic three-year approval.</p></details></section>
<section class="insight insight--assumption"><span class="insight-label">Short P&amp;L</span><h3>Budget the cost. Prove the benefit separately.</h3><p>Internal cost centre · €000s · illustrative annual expense model</p>
<div class="business-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Three-year internal product profit and loss"><table class="plan-pnl"><thead><tr><th scope="col">€000s</th><th scope="col">Y1</th><th scope="col">Y2</th><th scope="col">Y3</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Revenue</th><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Team</th><td>(960)</td><td>(960)</td><td>(960)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Infrastructure / API</th><td>(100)</td><td>(140)</td><td>(180)</td></tr>
<tr class="pnl-total"><th scope="row">Operating contribution</th><td>(1,060)</td><td>(1,100)</td><td>(1,140)</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="capacity-note"><strong>Capacity value · outside P&amp;L</strong><span>Y1 ~€0.58m → Y2 ~€0.97m → Y3 ~€1.16m</span><span>3-year cost: €3.30m · gross capacity value: ~€2.70m</span></div>
<details><summary>Model assumptions and investment conclusion</summary><p>Team: 8 FTE × €120k loaded annual cost = €960k/year, within the earlier custom-build staffing and cost range. Infrastructure/API: €100k / €140k / €180k as usage expands. Assume all development costs are expensed; no capitalization, depreciation, financing or tax model. No external sales or internal chargebacks. This is an incremental internal cost-centre view, not a company-wide P&amp;L.</p><p>The €80–150k pilot is assumed included in Year 1’s team/tool envelope, not added again. Confirm the same scope before approval. Major data remediation, VAT and whole-platform replacement remain excluded. Allocated staff expense is not necessarily incremental cash spending.</p><p>Capacity calculation: 600 scientists × 46 weeks × average adoption of 30% / 50% / 60% × 1 net hour saved per active scientist/week × assumed €70/hour. This yields 8,280 / 13,800 / 16,560 annual hours. The 1-hour net benefit is a hypothesis to validate after review effort, not an experimental result. Capacity is not revenue, payroll savings or realized cash benefit.</p><p>On these assumptions, gross three-year capacity value is about €2.70m against €3.30m of expense: capacity value alone does not justify custom ownership. Require evidenced additional scientific value, better economics or a lower-cost route before committing. Do not count research milestone gains twice. No cash payback or ROI is established by this model.</p></details></section>
